  <h2>How to use an average salary figure</h2>
  <p>
    An average is a weak benchmark for a single negotiation and a strong one for a trend. Used to
    judge whether a specific offer is fair, it misleads in both directions: it is pulled upward by
    a small number of very high earners, and it flattens the differences between industries that
    matter far more than the national figure. Used to see whether wages in a sector are moving,
    over several years and from a consistent source, it is reliable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The better comparison for an individual offer is the median for the same occupation in the
    same province, which Statistics Canada publishes for major occupational groups. It answers the
    question actually being asked, which is whether the employer is offering what the role
    typically pays, rather than how the role compares with every other job in the country.
  </p>
  <h2>Why province and industry outweigh the national figure</h2>
  <p>
    The provinces with the highest average wages are generally those with concentrated resource
    extraction or financial services, not those with the highest cost of living, and the two do
    not track each other. A salary that looks strong against the national average can be modest
    against local housing costs, and a salary that looks weak can go considerably further. Any
    comparison that ignores the cost side answers only half the question.
  </p>
  <p>
    Industry effects are larger still. The gap between median pay in the highest-paying and
    lowest-paying sectors exceeds the gap between the highest and lowest provinces by a wide
    margin. For anyone weighing a career move, the sector matters more than the address, which the
    national average obscures entirely by collapsing both into one number.
  </p>
